\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The weighted four-cycle Laplacian}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Use the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), with vertices
#{0,1,2,3}, edges #{e_i:i\to i+1}, and positive real weights
#{(1,t,1,t)}. Order the vertex and edge indicators as
#{(0,1,2,3)} and #{(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)}, with ordered-value maps
#{c_V:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^4} and
#{c_E:\mathbb R^E\to\mathbb R^4}. For a field with
#{f_c=c_Vf=(a,b,c,d)}, the incidence values are
#{c_EDf=(b-a,c-b,d-c,a-d)}. In these charts, the
[counting adjoint](fdip-0007) sends an edge field to
##{(c_VD^*c_E^{-1})(u_0,u_1,u_2,u_3)
  =(u_3-u_0,u_0-u_1,u_1-u_2,u_2-u_3).}
The weight map has array #{c_EW_tc_E^{-1}=\operatorname{diag}(1,t,1,t)}.
First multiply by #{W_t}, then apply #{D^*}. The resulting
#{L_t=D^*W_tD} has coordinate matrix
##{A_t=c_VL_tc_V^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
1+t&-1&0&-t\\
-1&1+t&-t&0\\
0&-t&1+t&-1\\
-t&0&-1&1+t
\end{pmatrix}.}}
\p{For example, its first coordinate is
#{t(a-d)-(b-a)=(1+t)a-b-td}; the other coordinates follow from
the same return formula. Thus
##{c_VL_t\delta_0=(1+t,-1,0,-t),\qquad L_t\mathbf1=0,}
and the counting energy is
##{\langle f,L_tf\rangle=f_c^TA_tf_c
=(b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2+(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2.}
The counting adjoint #{D^*} does not depend on #{t}. The weighted
return map #{D^*W_t}, and hence #{L_t}, do. When #{t=1} the two
return maps coincide; otherwise the factors #{t} must be retained.
Every row of the displayed array sums to zero. Since #{t>0}, the
energy vanishes exactly when all 4 field values agree. The
[positive-action comparison](fdip-001R) adds the mass term to this
edge energy.}
\p{This applies the [weighted incidence construction](fdip-0007)
to the four-cycle. The incidence convention follows
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.
The [cyclic energy factorization](fdip-0014) gives the corresponding
operator identity.}
